Why this is separate
--------------------

You are free to fork this code, modify it and ship it. Please do.

But ScreenSculpt is distributed as an unsigned application, which means users
cannot verify who built the copy they are running by checking a certificate.
The name and icon are, in practice, the only way someone can tell a genuine
build from a modified one. A fork that ships pixel-identical branding removes
that last signal — for an app that holds Screen Recording permission, that is a
real hazard rather than a theoretical one.

So: fork the code, and give your fork its own name and icon.

What you may do without asking
------------------------------

  * Use the name and icon to refer to this project (articles, reviews,
    comparisons, "works with ScreenSculpt", package listings).
  * Redistribute the unmodified application with its original branding.
  * Include screenshots of the application in documentation or reviews.

What needs permission
---------------------

  * Shipping a modified build under the ScreenSculpt name or icon.
  * Using the branding in a way that suggests endorsement or affiliation.

Ask by opening an issue. The answer is usually yes.
